---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Faq from '../../../components/Faq.astro';

const schemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'What Is a Good TDEE? Average TDEE by Age, Sex and Activity',
    description: 'There is no single good TDEE. See typical TDEE ranges by sex and activity, how age changes your number, and how to find the TDEE that is right for you.',
    url: 'https://tdeecheck.com/blog/average-tdee/',
    image: 'https://tdeecheck.com/images/average-tdee-hero.webp',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Farah Akram', jobTitle: 'Dietitian' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TDEE Check', url: 'https://tdeecheck.com/' },
    datePublished: '2026-10-22',
    dateModified: '2026-10-22',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is a normal TDEE for a woman?',
    a: 'Most women fall somewhere between about 1,600 and 2,600 kcal a day. A sedentary woman of average build usually maintains on roughly 1,600 to 1,800, a lightly active woman on 1,800 to 2,000, and a very active woman on 2,200 to 2,600. Your own height, weight and muscle mass can move you outside those bands, which is normal and not a problem to fix.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a normal TDEE for a man?',
    a: 'Most men fall between about 2,000 and 3,200 kcal a day. A sedentary man of average build typically maintains on 2,000 to 2,200, a moderately active man on 2,500 to 2,800, and a very active man on 2,800 to 3,200. Tall, heavy or muscular men regularly sit above the top of that range.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a higher TDEE better?',
    a: 'Not in itself. A high TDEE mostly reflects a bigger body, more muscle or a more physical day, and it does mean you can eat more while maintaining your weight, which many people enjoy. But it is not a health score. A smaller person with a TDEE of 1,700 is not doing worse than a larger person at 3,000; they simply run a smaller engine.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my TDEE lower than the average?',
    a: 'Usually for ordinary reasons: you are shorter or lighter than average, you carry less muscle, or your job keeps you seated. None of those are defects. A low TDEE only means your portions need to be sized to your body rather than to the table in this article.',
  },
  {
    q: 'At what age does TDEE fall the most?',
    a: 'The decline is gradual rather than sudden. From about age 30 it drifts down by roughly 1 to 2 percent per decade when muscle is lost, and the drift tends to steepen after 60 as activity and muscle mass fall together. Resistance training and adequate protein blunt most of it, because the main driver is muscle, not birthdays alone.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What Is a Good TDEE? Average TDEE by Age, Sex and Activity"
  description="There is no single good TDEE. See typical TDEE ranges by sex and activity, how age changes your number, and how to find the TDEE that is right for you."
  path="/blog/average-tdee/"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/images/average-tdee-hero.webp"
  schemas={schemas}
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Blog', path: '/blog/' },
    { name: 'Average TDEE', path: '/blog/average-tdee/' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-10">
    <p class="text-sm font-bold text-brand-700 uppercase tracking-wide">Guide · Reviewed by Farah Akram, Dietitian</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink-900">What is a good TDEE?</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      People ask this the way they ask what a good weight or a good salary is, hoping for one number they can aim at.
      TDEE does not work that way. There is no single good TDEE, because your total daily energy expenditure is
      simply the cost of running your particular body through your particular day. Averages are still useful, but
      as a sanity check rather than a target: they tell you whether your own number is in a plausible range for
      someone of your sex, size and activity. This guide gives you those ranges, shows how age moves them, and
      then explains why your own tested number matters more than any table, including this one.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/images/average-tdee-hero.webp"
      alt="Illustration of people of different ages, sizes and activity levels, each with their own daily calorie burn"
      width="2048"
      height="1152"
      class="mt-8 w-full rounded-2xl border border-brand-100"
      loading="eager"
    />

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">What the averages actually describe</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      When a table says the average woman maintains on about 2,000 kcal, it is describing an average build living
      an average day. Three things do almost all of the work in that number. The first is body size: a larger body
      has more tissue to maintain and more mass to move, so it burns more at every activity level. The second is
      sex, mostly because men on average carry more muscle and less fat at the same weight, and muscle is the
      hungrier tissue. The third is activity, which is the one part fully under your control. Notice what is not
      on the list: worth, fitness or discipline. TDEE measures the size of the engine, not the quality of the
      driver.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Typical TDEE ranges by sex and activity</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      These are typical ranges for adults of average build, meaning average height and a healthy to slightly
      overweight body composition. They are descriptions of what is common, not standards you are supposed to
      meet, and plenty of healthy people sit outside them in both directions.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-brand-100 bg-white">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-brand-50 text-left">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-extrabold text-ink-900">Activity level</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-extrabold text-ink-900">Women (kcal per day)</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-extrabold text-ink-900">Men (kcal per day)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-brand-50">
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Sedentary</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,600-1,800</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,000-2,200</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Lightly active</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,800-2,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,200-2,500</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Moderately active</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,000-2,200</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,500-2,800</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Very active</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,200-2,600</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2,800-3,200</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Read your row loosely. If you are taller, heavier or more muscular than average, expect the top of your band
      or above it. If you are smaller, expect the bottom. The table is a map of the neighborhood, not your address.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      The edges deserve a mention too. Competitive endurance athletes and people in heavy manual jobs can burn well
      above the very active row, sometimes past 3,500 kcal for women and 4,000 for men during peak training or work
      periods. At the other end, a small, older, fully sedentary adult can maintain below 1,600. Both are ordinary
      bodies in unusual weeks, not exceptions that break the table.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Two real numbers, worked out</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Averages become clearer with actual people. The example used across this site is a woman of 32, 5 ft 6 in and
      165 lb who exercises three to five days a week. Her BMR is 1,475 kcal, and at the moderate multiplier her
      TDEE is <strong>2,287 kcal</strong>, near the top of the moderately active band for women, because she is a
      little taller and heavier than the average build the table assumes. Her floor number comes from the same
      equation our <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/bmr-calculator/">BMR
      Calculator</a> uses. The men's example on this site, a man of 28, 5 ft 10 in and 185 lb who trains hard most
      days, lands at <strong>3,132 kcal</strong>, inside the very active band for men. Neither number is better
      than the other. Each is simply correct for the body and the week it describes.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">How age lowers TDEE</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      TDEE tends to drift down with age, but not because the calendar directly slows your cells. The main mechanism
      is muscle. From about age 30, adults who do not train lose muscle gradually, roughly enough to trim 1 to 2
      percent from daily burn per decade, and since muscle is what your
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/blog/tdee-vs-bmr/">BMR</a> mostly pays for, the resting
      number falls with it. Everyday movement usually falls in the same decades: jobs get more senior and more
      seated, children grow up, steps quietly halve. The encouraging part is that both drivers respond to behavior.
      People who keep resistance training and stay active into their fifties and sixties keep most of their muscle,
      and with it most of their TDEE. Age moves the number mainly by moving the inputs, and the inputs are partly
      yours to choose.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Why comparing yourself to the average misleads</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      The average person in the table does not exist. Real bodies spread widely around every row, because height
      alone can add or remove several hundred calories, and two people of identical height, weight and sex can
      still differ through muscle mass and how much they move outside exercise. Comparing your number to the table
      and finding it lower tells you almost nothing, yet it regularly convinces people their metabolism is broken
      when it is simply sized to their body. The only question that matters is whether your weight holds steady at
      your number, and only your own tracking can answer that.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Find your own number instead</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Use the table to check plausibility, then get personal. Run your own stats through our
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/">TDEE Calculator</a>, eat near the result for two to
      three weeks, and watch your weekly average weight. Flat means you have found your real maintenance, whatever
      the tables say. Drifting means your number sits a little above or below the estimate, and a small adjustment
      closes the gap. That tested figure, not an average for your age group, is the good TDEE: yours.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-8 rounded-xl bg-brand-50 border border-brand-100 px-5 py-4 text-sm text-ink-700">
      Reviewed by <strong>Farah Akram, Dietitian</strong>. Estimates only, not medical advice. See the
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/medical-disclaimer/">medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>

    <Faq faqs={faqs} title="Average TDEE questions" />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
